    const medicalExpansion=[
      ['Endometriosis','Internal Medicine','Tissue similar to the uterine lining grows outside the uterus and can cause inflammation, scarring, pelvic pain, painful periods, pain with sex, bowel or bladder symptoms, and infertility. The exact cause is not known. Evaluation begins with history and pelvic examination; ultrasound is usually the first imaging test, while MRI may help map extensive disease. Normal imaging does not exclude endometriosis, and laparoscopy may confirm and treat it when needed. Treatment is individualized and may include anti-inflammatory pain medicines, combined hormonal contraception, progestin therapy, GnRH medicines with add-back therapy, fertility treatment, or laparoscopic surgery. Hysterectomy is not automatically required and does not guarantee that all symptoms will resolve.','English'],
      ['Iron-deficiency anemia','Blood','Iron deficiency means the body does not have enough iron to make normal hemoglobin. Common causes include menstrual or gastrointestinal blood loss, pregnancy, low intake, and poor absorption. Testing commonly includes a CBC, ferritin, iron studies, reticulocyte count, and evaluation for the source of blood loss; gastrointestinal investigation depends on age, symptoms, sex, and risk. Treatment replaces iron by mouth or intravenously and corrects the cause. Severe symptoms, chest pain, fainting, active bleeding, or very low hemoglobin require urgent assessment.','English'],
      ['Neutropenia','Blood','Neutropenia is a low neutrophil count, reducing part of the body’s defense against infection. Causes include medicines and chemotherapy, viral illness, autoimmune disease, nutritional deficiency, enlarged spleen, and bone-marrow disorders. Evaluation uses the absolute neutrophil count, repeat CBC with differential, medication and infection review, blood smear, and selected nutritional, immune, infectious, or marrow tests. Treatment targets the cause and may include observation, stopping an offending medicine, antibiotics for infection, or growth-factor support. Fever with significant neutropenia is an emergency.','English'],
      ['Leukocytosis','Blood','Leukocytosis means an elevated white-blood-cell count. Infection, inflammation, stress, smoking, corticosteroids, pregnancy, and marrow disorders are possible causes. The pattern on the differential count, symptoms, prior results, medication history, and peripheral smear help direct testing; persistent or unexplained abnormalities may require flow cytometry, molecular testing, or bone-marrow examination. Treatment is aimed at the cause rather than the number alone.','English'],
      ['Eosinophilia','Blood','Eosinophilia is an increased number of eosinophils. Allergic disease, asthma, drug reactions, parasites, autoimmune conditions, adrenal disorders, and some blood cancers can cause it. Evaluation is guided by travel, exposures, medicines, symptoms, CBC trend, blood smear, organ testing, and selected infectious or molecular studies. Treatment addresses the cause; marked eosinophilia with heart, lung, neurologic, or other organ symptoms needs prompt specialist evaluation.','English'],
      ['Unexplained lymph nodes','Blood','Lymph nodes may enlarge from infection, inflammation, autoimmune disease, medicines, or cancer. Evaluation considers location, tenderness, duration, growth, fever, night sweats, weight loss, exposures, and examination. Testing may include CBC, targeted infection studies, ultrasound or CT, and biopsy when nodes are persistent, enlarging, unusually firm, or otherwise concerning. An excisional biopsy is sometimes preferred when lymphoma is suspected because architecture matters. Treatment depends on the diagnosis.','English'],
      ['Enlarged spleen','Blood','Splenomegaly means the spleen is larger than expected. Liver disease, infection, hemolysis, blood cancers, inflammatory disease, and blood-flow problems are possible causes. Evaluation may include CBC, blood smear, liver tests, hemolysis studies, ultrasound or CT, and disease-specific testing. Treatment addresses the underlying cause. Sudden severe left-upper-abdominal or shoulder pain, dizziness, or fainting can signal rupture and requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Abnormal uterine bleeding','Internal Medicine','Bleeding that is unusually heavy, prolonged, frequent, irregular, or occurs after menopause deserves evaluation. Causes include pregnancy, fibroids, polyps, hormonal or ovulatory changes, adenomyosis, medicines, bleeding disorders, and precancer or cancer. Testing may include a pregnancy test, CBC and iron studies, pelvic examination, ultrasound, and endometrial sampling based on age and risk. Treatment may use iron, hormonal therapy, tranexamic acid, procedures, or surgery according to the cause and the patient’s goals. Heavy bleeding with fainting, chest pain, or shortness of breath needs urgent care.','English'],
      ['Developmental milestones','Internal Medicine','Developmental milestones explained in plain language, including typical patterns, screening, normal variation, early warning signs, and when pediatric evaluation may be helpful.','English'],
      ['Anemia por deficiencia de hierro','Español','Significa que no hay suficiente hierro para producir hemoglobina normal. Las causas incluyen sangrado menstrual o digestivo, embarazo, alimentación insuficiente y mala absorción. Las pruebas suelen incluir hemograma, ferritina, estudios de hierro, reticulocitos y búsqueda de la fuente del sangrado. El tratamiento repone hierro por vía oral o intravenosa y corrige la causa. Síntomas graves o sangrado activo requieren evaluación urgente.','Español'],
      ['Sports physicals','Internal Medicine','Sports physicals explained through health history, examination, safe participation, injury prevention, cardiac warning signs, and questions to discuss with a qualified clinician.','English'],
      ['Leucocitosis','Español','Significa un recuento elevado de glóbulos blancos. Infección, inflamación, estrés, tabaco, corticosteroides, embarazo y trastornos de médula ósea son causas posibles. El diferencial, los síntomas, resultados previos y el frotis orientan las pruebas; una alteración persistente puede requerir citometría de flujo, pruebas moleculares o estudio de médula. Se trata la causa, no solamente el número.','Español'],
      ['Eosinofilia','Español','Es un aumento de eosinófilos. Alergias, asma, reacciones a medicamentos, parásitos, enfermedades autoinmunes y algunos cánceres de sangre pueden causarla. La evaluación depende de viajes, exposiciones, medicamentos, síntomas, tendencia del hemograma, frotis y pruebas dirigidas. La eosinofilia marcada con daño de órganos requiere evaluación rápida.','Español'],
      ['Ganglios linfáticos aumentados','Español','Los ganglios pueden aumentar por infección, inflamación, enfermedad autoinmune, medicamentos o cáncer. La ubicación, duración, crecimiento, fiebre, sudoración nocturna, pérdida de peso y examen orientan las pruebas. Puede ser necesario un hemograma, estudios de infección, imágenes o biopsia. Cuando se sospecha linfoma, a veces se prefiere extraer un ganglio completo.','Español'],
      ['Bazo aumentado','Español','La esplenomegalia significa que el bazo está aumentado. Enfermedad hepática, infecciones, hemólisis, cánceres de sangre, inflamación y problemas del flujo sanguíneo pueden causarla. La evaluación puede incluir hemograma, frotis, pruebas hepáticas, estudios de hemólisis e imágenes. Dolor intenso repentino en el abdomen superior izquierdo o el hombro, mareo o desmayo requiere atención de emergencia.','Español'],
      ['Sangrado uterino anormal','Español','El sangrado muy abundante, prolongado, frecuente, irregular o después de la menopausia requiere evaluación. Embarazo, fibromas, pólipos, cambios hormonales, adenomiosis, medicamentos, trastornos hemorrágicos y cáncer son causas posibles. Las pruebas pueden incluir embarazo, hemograma, hierro, examen pélvico, ultrasonido y biopsia endometrial según edad y riesgo. El tratamiento depende de la causa y puede incluir hierro, hormonas, ácido tranexámico, procedimientos o cirugía.','Español']
    ];
    const prioritySearchExpansion=[
      ['Chest pain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: discomfort anywhere in the chest. Causes range from muscle strain and reflux to heart attack or pulmonary embolism. Tests may include ECG, troponin, chest imaging, and risk-directed studies. Treatment depends on the cause. New pressure, sweating, fainting, or breathlessness requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Shortness of breath','Internal Medicine','Meaning: difficult or uncomfortable breathing. Common causes include asthma, infection, anemia, heart failure, COPD, clots, and anxiety. Tests may include oxygen measurement, examination, CBC, ECG, chest imaging, and lung or heart studies. Treatment targets the cause; severe or sudden symptoms require emergency care.','English'],
      ['Abdominal pain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pain between the chest and pelvis. Causes include indigestion, infection, constipation, gallbladder, appendix, pancreas, urinary, and gynecologic disorders. Testing is guided by location and symptoms and may include blood, urine, pregnancy testing, ultrasound, or CT. Severe persistent pain or rigidity needs urgent care.','English'],
      ['Headache','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pain involving the head or upper neck. Migraine and tension headache are common, but infection, bleeding, glaucoma, or high blood pressure can be serious causes. Testing depends on examination and red flags. Sudden worst-ever headache, weakness, confusion, fever, or vision loss requires urgent evaluation.','English'],
      ['Dizziness and vertigo','Internal Medicine','Meaning: lightheadedness, imbalance, or a spinning sensation. Causes include dehydration, inner-ear disease, medicines, anemia, heart rhythm problems, and neurologic disease. Evaluation may include orthostatic vital signs, ear and neurologic examination, ECG, blood tests, or imaging. New stroke symptoms require emergency care.','English'],
      ['Fatigue','Internal Medicine','Meaning: persistent lack of energy not relieved by usual rest. Causes include sleep problems, anemia, thyroid disease, infection, depression, medicines, heart or lung disease, and cancer. Evaluation uses history, examination, and targeted blood tests. Treatment addresses the cause, sleep, activity, nutrition, and contributing medicines.','English'],
      ['Fever','Internal Medicine','Meaning: elevated body temperature, usually from infection or inflammation. Evaluation depends on duration, exposures, immune status, symptoms, examination, and selected cultures, blood tests, or imaging. Treatment may include fluids, fever control, and cause-specific therapy. Fever during chemotherapy or severe immune suppression is an emergency.','English'],
      ['Persistent cough','Internal Medicine','Meaning: cough lasting longer than expected. Causes include viral illness, asthma, reflux, postnasal drainage, medicines, smoking, COPD, infection, and cancer. Evaluation may include chest imaging and lung testing. Treatment targets the cause. Coughing blood, low oxygen, chest pain, or severe breathlessness requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Back pain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pain involving the spine, muscles, nerves, or nearby organs. Strain is common, but fracture, infection, kidney disease, or cancer can occur. Testing depends on red flags and may include imaging. New weakness, saddle numbness, bladder changes, fever, or cancer-associated severe pain needs urgent evaluation.','English'],
      ['Joint pain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pain in one or more joints. Injury, osteoarthritis, gout, infection, and autoimmune inflammation are common causes. Evaluation may include examination, blood tests, imaging, or joint-fluid analysis. Treatment may use activity modification, physical therapy, pain control, or disease-specific therapy. A hot swollen joint with fever is urgent.','English'],
      ['Nausea and vomiting','Internal Medicine','Meaning: queasiness with or without emptying the stomach. Causes include infection, medicines, migraine, pregnancy, bowel obstruction, and metabolic disease. Testing depends on duration and dehydration risk. Treatment includes fluids and cause-specific therapy. Blood, severe pain, confusion, or inability to keep fluids down needs prompt care.','English'],
      ['Diarrhea','Internal Medicine','Meaning: frequent loose stools. Causes include infection, medicines, food intolerance, inflammation, malabsorption, and cancer treatment. Testing is guided by duration, travel, blood, fever, and immune status. Treatment emphasizes hydration and the cause. Bloody stool, severe dehydration, high fever, or immunotherapy-associated diarrhea requires prompt assessment.','English'],
      ['Constipation','Internal Medicine','Meaning: infrequent, hard, or difficult stools. Low fiber, dehydration, inactivity, medicines, thyroid disease, neurologic disease, and obstruction are possible causes. Evaluation may include examination and age-appropriate colorectal assessment. Treatment uses fluids, fiber when appropriate, activity, laxatives, and cause correction. Vomiting with distention is urgent.','English'],
      ['Abdominal bloating','Internal Medicine','Meaning: fullness or visible abdominal swelling. Diet, constipation, intolerance, irritable bowel syndrome, fluid, ovarian disease, and other conditions may contribute. Evaluation depends on persistence and associated symptoms. Treatment addresses the cause. Progressive swelling, weight loss, vomiting, bleeding, or early satiety deserves medical evaluation.','English'],
      ['Unintentional weight loss','Internal Medicine','Meaning: losing weight without trying. Causes include thyroid disease, diabetes, depression, gastrointestinal disease, infection, medication effects, and cancer. Evaluation includes diet and medication review, examination, basic blood tests, and symptom-directed testing. Treatment addresses the cause and nutrition. Rapid or progressive loss needs prompt assessment.','English'],
      ['Unexpected weight gain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: weight increase not explained by intended diet change. Causes include fluid retention, thyroid disease, medicines, sleep problems, and reduced activity. Evaluation may include examination, medication review, metabolic tests, and heart, kidney, or liver assessment. Sudden gain with breathlessness or swelling needs timely care.','English'],
      ['Night sweats','Internal Medicine','Meaning: sweating that soaks sleepwear or bedding. Menopause, medicines, infection, sleep disorders, endocrine disease, lymphoma, and other conditions are possible causes. Evaluation considers fever, weight loss, nodes, exposures, medicines, CBC, and targeted testing. Persistent drenching sweats should be assessed.','English'],
      ['Easy bruising','Blood','Meaning: bruises appearing often or after minimal trauma. Causes include fragile skin, medicines, low platelets, liver disease, vitamin deficiency, and clotting disorders. Tests may include CBC, blood smear, PT/INR, aPTT, liver studies, and selected factor testing. Major spontaneous bleeding, severe headache, or weakness is urgent.','English'],
      ['Blood in stool','Internal Medicine','Meaning: visible red blood or black tarry stool. Causes include hemorrhoids, fissures, ulcers, diverticular disease, inflammation, polyps, and cancer. Evaluation may include examination, CBC, stool assessment, endoscopy, or colonoscopy. Heavy bleeding, fainting, rapid heartbeat, or black stool with weakness requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Blood in urine','Internal Medicine','Meaning: red blood cells in urine, visible or microscopic. Infection, stones, prostate disease, kidney disease, medicines, trauma, and urinary cancer are possible causes. Evaluation may include urinalysis, culture, kidney tests, imaging, and cystoscopy. Clots, inability to urinate, fever, or severe pain needs prompt care.','English'],
      ['Painful urination','Internal Medicine','Meaning: burning or pain while urinating. Urinary infection is common, but stones, irritation, prostate disease, and sexually transmitted infection can cause it. Evaluation usually includes urinalysis and sometimes culture or infection testing. Treatment targets the cause. Fever, flank pain, pregnancy, or immune suppression needs timely assessment.','English'],
      ['Leg swelling','Internal Medicine','Meaning: fluid or inflammation causing one or both legs to enlarge. Causes include vein disease, medicines, heart, kidney, or liver disease, infection, and blood clots. Evaluation may include ultrasound and organ testing. Sudden one-sided swelling, chest pain, or breathlessness requires urgent evaluation.','English'],
      ['Skin rash','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a change in skin color, texture, or sensation. Causes include irritation, allergy, infection, autoimmune disease, medicines, and cancer therapy. Evaluation uses appearance, distribution, exposures, and sometimes blood tests or biopsy. Blistering, facial swelling, breathing trouble, or mucosal sores requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Itching','Internal Medicine','Meaning: an urge to scratch, with or without visible rash. Dry skin, allergy, medicines, liver or kidney disease, thyroid disease, iron deficiency, and blood disorders may contribute. Evaluation uses skin examination and targeted tests. Treatment includes skin care and cause-directed therapy. Jaundice or systemic symptoms need assessment.','English'],
      ['Numbness and tingling','Internal Medicine','Meaning: reduced or abnormal sensation. Compression, diabetes, vitamin deficiency, thyroid disease, medicines, chemotherapy, and neurologic disorders are possible causes. Evaluation may include neurologic examination, blood tests, and nerve studies. Sudden one-sided symptoms, weakness, speech difficulty, or bladder changes requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Heart palpitations','Internal Medicine','Meaning: awareness of fast, irregular, or forceful heartbeats. Stress, caffeine, anemia, thyroid disease, medicines, and arrhythmias can cause them. Evaluation may include ECG, rhythm monitoring, CBC, electrolytes, and thyroid tests. Palpitations with chest pain, fainting, or severe breathlessness require urgent care.','English'],
      ['Fainting','Internal Medicine','Meaning: brief loss of consciousness from reduced brain blood flow. Dehydration, reflex fainting, medicines, bleeding, low glucose, and heart rhythm problems are possible causes. Evaluation includes history, vital signs, ECG, and targeted testing. Fainting during exercise, with chest pain, injury, or abnormal rhythm is urgent.','English'],
      ['Swollen lymph nodes','Blood','Meaning: enlarged immune-system glands, often in the neck, armpit, or groin. Infection is common; autoimmune disease, medicines, and cancer are other causes. Evaluation considers duration, location, symptoms, CBC, imaging, and sometimes biopsy. Persistent, hard, growing nodes or associated weight loss and sweats require assessment.','English'],
      ['Loss of appetite','Internal Medicine','Meaning: reduced desire to eat. Infection, medicines, mood disorders, digestive disease, pain, metabolic illness, and cancer can contribute. Evaluation includes weight trend, medication review, examination, and targeted tests. Treatment addresses the cause and nutrition. Dehydration, rapid weight loss, or inability to eat needs prompt help.','English'],
      ['Difficulty swallowing','Internal Medicine','Meaning: food or liquid feels difficult to move from mouth to stomach. Reflux injury, narrowing, motility disorders, neurologic disease, and cancer are possible causes. Evaluation may include swallow study, endoscopy, or imaging. Food impaction, inability to swallow saliva, choking, or progressive weight loss requires urgent evaluation.','English'],
      ['Hoarseness','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a persistent change in voice quality. Infection, voice strain, reflux, smoking, nerve injury, thyroid disease, and head-and-neck cancer are possible causes. Examination of the vocal cords is considered when symptoms persist. Breathing difficulty or rapidly worsening neck swelling is urgent.','English'],
      ['Rectal bleeding','Internal Medicine','Meaning: red or dark blood passed from the rectum. Hemorrhoids and fissures are common, but inflammation, diverticular disease, polyps, and cancer must be considered. Evaluation may include examination, CBC, and colonoscopy. Heavy bleeding, dizziness, fainting, or black stool requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Pelvic pain','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pain below the abdomen. Menstrual disorders, endometriosis, ovarian cysts, infection, urinary disease, bowel disease, and pregnancy complications are possible causes. Evaluation may include pregnancy testing, pelvic examination, urine tests, ultrasound, or other imaging. Sudden severe pain, fainting, fever, or pregnancy is urgent.','English'],
      ['Breast lump','Cancer','Meaning: a new mass or thickening in breast tissue. Cysts and fibroadenomas are common, but cancer must be excluded. Evaluation uses clinical examination and age-appropriate ultrasound, mammography, and sometimes biopsy. Treatment depends on the diagnosis. Skin dimpling, bloody discharge, or a persistent mass needs timely assessment.','English'],
      ['Lung nodule','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a small spot seen on chest imaging. Most are benign scars or granulomas, but some represent early cancer. Size, shape, growth, smoking history, and prior imaging guide follow-up with CT, PET, biopsy, or surveillance. Treatment ranges from observation to surgery or cancer therapy.','English'],
      ['Thyroid nodule','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a lump within the thyroid. Most are benign. Evaluation includes examination, TSH, ultrasound risk features, and fine-needle biopsy when indicated. Treatment may involve observation, thyroid medicine for functional disease, radioiodine, ablation, or surgery. Rapid growth, hoarseness, or breathing difficulty needs prompt evaluation.','English'],
      ['High blood sugar','Internal Medicine','Meaning: glucose above the expected range. Diabetes, acute illness, steroids, and endocrine disorders can cause it. Tests include repeat fasting glucose, A1C, and sometimes glucose-tolerance or antibody testing. Treatment uses nutrition, activity, medicines, or insulin. Vomiting, dehydration, confusion, or very high readings can be emergent.','English'],
      ['Low blood sugar','Internal Medicine','Meaning: glucose low enough to cause symptoms or harm. Diabetes medicines, missed meals, alcohol, organ disease, and hormonal disorders are causes. Confirm with glucose testing and review timing and medicines. Treat promptly with fast carbohydrate when safe; severe confusion, seizure, or inability to swallow requires emergency glucagon and help.','English'],
      ['High cholesterol','Internal Medicine','Meaning: unhealthy levels of LDL or other blood lipids that increase cardiovascular risk. Genetics, diet, diabetes, thyroid disease, and medicines contribute. Testing uses a lipid panel and overall risk assessment. Treatment includes lifestyle measures and risk-based statin or other lipid-lowering therapy.','English'],
      ['Acid reflux','Internal Medicine','Meaning: stomach contents move into the esophagus, causing burning, regurgitation, cough, or throat symptoms. Evaluation is often clinical; endoscopy or pH testing is used for alarms or persistent symptoms. Treatment includes meal and weight measures, acid suppression, and selected procedures. Trouble swallowing or bleeding needs evaluation.','English'],
      ['Urinary tract infection','Internal Medicine','Meaning: infection of the bladder or urinary system. Bacteria are the usual cause. Urinalysis and culture are used according to symptoms and risk. Treatment may include antibiotics and hydration. Fever, flank pain, vomiting, pregnancy, obstruction, or immune suppression raises concern for complicated infection.','English'],
      ['Kidney stones','Internal Medicine','Meaning: crystals form a stone in the urinary tract. Dehydration, diet, genetics, infection, and metabolic disorders contribute. Evaluation may include urine testing, kidney function, and ultrasound or CT. Treatment includes pain control, fluids, passage support, or a procedure. Fever with obstruction is an emergency.','English'],
      ['Gallstones','Internal Medicine','Meaning: hardened material forms in the gallbladder. Many cause no symptoms; obstruction can cause right-upper-abdominal pain, inflammation, jaundice, or pancreatitis. Ultrasound and liver tests are common tests. Symptomatic disease often requires gallbladder surgery. Fever, jaundice, or persistent severe pain needs urgent assessment.','English'],
      ['Migraine','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a neurologic disorder causing recurrent headache, often with nausea, light or sound sensitivity, and sometimes aura. Diagnosis is clinical after red flags are excluded. Treatment includes trigger management, acute medicines, and preventive therapy when frequent. New neurologic deficits or abrupt worst-ever pain requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Asthma','Internal Medicine','Meaning: inflamed, variable airways cause wheeze, cough, chest tightness, and breathlessness. Allergens, infections, exercise, smoke, and work exposures can trigger symptoms. Spirometry helps confirm diagnosis. Treatment centers on inhaled anti-inflammatory therapy with individualized relievers. Severe breathing difficulty or poor response needs urgent care.','English'],
      ['Pneumonia','Internal Medicine','Meaning: infection inflames the air sacs of the lungs. Viruses, bacteria, and aspiration are common causes. Evaluation uses vital signs, oxygen level, examination, and often chest imaging. Treatment may include supportive care, antibiotics when appropriate, and oxygen. Low oxygen, confusion, or breathing distress requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Sleep apnea','Internal Medicine','Meaning: breathing repeatedly narrows or stops during sleep. Snoring, airway anatomy, weight, and neurologic factors contribute. Diagnosis uses a home or laboratory sleep study. Treatment may include weight management, positive-airway-pressure therapy, oral devices, positional measures, or surgery. Untreated disease raises cardiovascular and accident risk.','English'],
      ['Anxiety','Internal Medicine','Meaning: excessive fear or worry that affects daily life. Medical conditions, medicines, substances, stress, and anxiety disorders may contribute. Evaluation includes safety, symptom pattern, and selected medical tests. Treatment may include psychotherapy, sleep and activity support, and medicines. Suicidal thoughts or inability to stay safe requires immediate help.','English'],
      ['Depression','Internal Medicine','Meaning: persistent low mood or loss of interest with changes in sleep, energy, appetite, concentration, or hope. Medical illness, medicines, stress, and depressive disorders contribute. Screening and clinical assessment guide care. Treatment includes psychotherapy, medicines, social support, and urgent safety planning when needed.','English'],
      ['Osteoporosis','Internal Medicine','Meaning: weakened bone strength increases fracture risk. Aging, menopause, steroids, low weight, endocrine disease, and some cancer treatments contribute. Bone-density testing and fracture-risk assessment guide care. Treatment includes exercise, calcium and vitamin D adequacy, fall prevention, and risk-based bone medicines.','English'],
      ['Arthritis','Internal Medicine','Meaning: joint pain, stiffness, or inflammation from osteoarthritis, autoimmune disease, crystals, infection, or injury. Evaluation uses pattern, examination, imaging, and selected blood or joint-fluid tests. Treatment ranges from exercise and pain control to disease-modifying therapy. A hot swollen joint with fever is urgent.','English'],
      ['Gout','Internal Medicine','Meaning: urate crystals cause sudden inflammatory arthritis, commonly in the big toe, ankle, or knee. Kidney disease, genetics, medicines, and diet influence risk. Joint-fluid crystal analysis is most definitive. Treatment includes anti-inflammatory therapy for attacks and urate-lowering medicine for appropriate patients.','English'],
      ['Hypothyroidism','Internal Medicine','Meaning: the thyroid does not produce enough hormone. Autoimmune thyroiditis, surgery, radiation, and some medicines are causes. TSH and free T4 establish the diagnosis. Treatment is usually levothyroxine with laboratory-guided dosing. Severe confusion, low temperature, or marked slowing requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Hyperthyroidism','Internal Medicine','Meaning: excess thyroid hormone speeds body functions. Graves disease, toxic nodules, thyroiditis, and medicines are causes. TSH, thyroid hormones, antibodies, and selected imaging guide diagnosis. Treatment may include symptom control, antithyroid medicine, radioiodine, or surgery. Severe fever, agitation, or rapid rhythm is emergent.','English'],
      ['Atrial fibrillation','Internal Medicine','Meaning: an irregular upper-heart rhythm that can cause palpitations, fatigue, breathlessness, and stroke risk. ECG confirms it; thyroid, electrolyte, heart, and sleep evaluation may follow. Treatment addresses rate or rhythm and individualized anticoagulation. Chest pain, fainting, or stroke symptoms requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['Heart failure','Internal Medicine','Meaning: the heart cannot fill or pump efficiently enough for the body’s needs. Coronary disease, high blood pressure, valve disease, and cardiomyopathy are causes. BNP, ECG, chest imaging, and echocardiography help evaluate it. Treatment uses cause-directed medicines, fluid management, devices, and lifestyle support.','English'],
      ['Coronary artery disease','Internal Medicine','Meaning: plaque narrows arteries supplying the heart. Cholesterol, smoking, diabetes, high blood pressure, age, and genetics contribute. Evaluation uses risk assessment, ECG, stress testing, CT, or angiography as appropriate. Treatment includes lifestyle, medicines, and selected stents or bypass surgery. Acute chest pressure is emergent.','English'],
      ['Peripheral artery disease','Internal Medicine','Meaning: narrowed arteries reduce blood flow, usually to the legs. Smoking, diabetes, cholesterol, and age raise risk. An ankle-brachial index and vascular imaging may be used. Treatment includes walking therapy, risk-factor medicines, and selected procedures. Sudden cold painful limb or tissue loss is urgent.','English'],
      ['Deep-vein thrombosis','Blood','Meaning: a clot forms in a deep vein, commonly in the leg. Surgery, immobility, estrogen, pregnancy, cancer, and inherited factors raise risk. Ultrasound and sometimes D-dimer guide diagnosis. Treatment usually uses anticoagulation. New chest pain, coughing blood, fainting, or breathlessness suggests pulmonary embolism and is emergent.','English'],
      ['Pulmonary embolism','Blood','Meaning: a clot blocks blood flow in the lungs, often after traveling from a leg vein. Risk assessment, oxygen measurement, ECG, D-dimer, CT angiography, or ventilation-perfusion scanning may be used. Treatment ranges from anticoagulation to urgent clot-directed therapy. Sudden symptoms require emergency care.','English'],
      ['Stroke warning signs','Internal Medicine','Meaning: sudden brain dysfunction from blocked or bleeding blood vessels. Facial droop, arm weakness, speech trouble, vision loss, imbalance, or severe headache are warnings. Immediate brain and vessel imaging guides time-sensitive treatment. Call emergency services immediately; do not drive or wait for symptoms to resolve.','English'],
      ['Transient ischemic attack','Internal Medicine','Meaning: temporary stroke-like symptoms from reduced brain blood flow. Symptoms may resolve but predict early stroke risk. Urgent brain and vessel imaging, ECG, rhythm monitoring, and risk-factor testing are needed. Treatment may include antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy and vascular risk reduction based on the cause.','English'],
      ['Chronic kidney disease stages','Internal Medicine','Meaning: kidney damage or reduced filtration persists at least three months. Diabetes and high blood pressure are leading causes. eGFR, urine albumin, urinalysis, imaging, and cause-directed tests guide staging. Treatment controls the cause, blood pressure, diabetes, cardiovascular risk, and medication safety while planning advanced care when needed.','English'],
      ['Liver enzyme elevation','Internal Medicine','Meaning: ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, or bilirubin is above range. Fatty liver, alcohol, medicines, infection, obstruction, autoimmune disease, and muscle injury are possible causes. Repeat patterns, history, blood tests, and ultrasound guide evaluation. Jaundice, confusion, severe pain, or bleeding requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Fatty liver','Internal Medicine','Meaning: excess fat accumulates in the liver, often with metabolic risk factors. Evaluation uses liver tests, metabolic assessment, ultrasound, and noninvasive fibrosis scores or elastography. Treatment emphasizes gradual weight loss, physical activity, diabetes and lipid control, alcohol review, and specialist care for significant fibrosis.','English'],
      ['Hepatitis B','Internal Medicine','Meaning: hepatitis B virus infects the liver and may become chronic. HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs, HBV DNA, liver tests, and fibrosis assessment define status. Treatment depends on viral activity and liver risk; monitoring also addresses liver cancer. Vaccination prevents infection. Acute liver-failure symptoms require urgent care.','English'],
      ['Hepatitis C','Internal Medicine','Meaning: hepatitis C virus can cause chronic liver inflammation, cirrhosis, and liver cancer. An antibody screen is followed by RNA testing for active infection. Direct-acting antiviral tablets cure most infections. Fibrosis stage guides follow-up. Prevention includes avoiding blood exposure; no vaccine currently exists.','English'],
      ['Irritable bowel syndrome','Internal Medicine','Meaning: recurrent abdominal pain with altered bowel habits without visible structural disease. Gut sensitivity, motility, diet, and stress interact. Diagnosis uses symptom criteria after checking alarm features and selected tests. Treatment may include dietary changes, fiber, bowel-directed medicines, and brain-gut therapies. Bleeding or weight loss needs evaluation.','English'],
      ['Inflammatory bowel disease','Internal Medicine','Meaning: Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis cause chronic immune-mediated digestive inflammation. Blood and stool tests, colonoscopy with biopsy, and imaging establish extent. Treatment may use anti-inflammatory, immune, biologic, or targeted medicines and sometimes surgery. Severe bleeding, dehydration, fever, or distention requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Celiac disease','Internal Medicine','Meaning: gluten triggers immune injury to the small intestine in genetically susceptible people. Testing begins with celiac antibodies while still eating gluten and may include endoscopic biopsy. Treatment is a lifelong gluten-free diet with nutrition monitoring. Starting restriction before testing can obscure the diagnosis.','English'],
      ['Diverticulitis','Internal Medicine','Meaning: pouches in the colon become inflamed, often causing left-lower-abdominal pain and fever. Examination, blood tests, and CT are used according to severity. Treatment ranges from fluids and pain control to antibiotics, drainage, or surgery. Peritonitis, sepsis, obstruction, or inability to drink requires urgent care.','English'],
      ['Hemorrhoids','Internal Medicine','Meaning: swollen veins in the anus or rectum can cause bleeding, itching, or discomfort. Diagnosis usually follows examination. Treatment includes fiber, fluids, bowel-habit changes, topical relief, and office or surgical procedures when needed. Rectal bleeding should not automatically be attributed to hemorrhoids without appropriate evaluation.','English'],
      ['Polycystic ovary syndrome','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a hormonal-metabolic condition involving irregular ovulation, androgen excess, and sometimes polycystic ovaries. Diagnosis excludes pregnancy, thyroid, prolactin, and related disorders. Treatment is goal-based and may include lifestyle support, cycle protection, contraception, metformin, hair treatments, or ovulation therapy.','English'],
      ['Uterine fibroids','Internal Medicine','Meaning: benign muscle tumors of the uterus may cause heavy bleeding, pressure, pain, anemia, or fertility problems. Pelvic examination and ultrasound usually establish the diagnosis. Treatment ranges from observation and medicines to embolization, focused procedures, myomectomy, or hysterectomy according to symptoms and goals.','English'],
      ['Ovarian cyst','Internal Medicine','Meaning: a fluid-filled or complex ovarian structure, often related to normal ovulation. Ultrasound appearance, size, symptoms, age, and menopausal status guide follow-up and selected blood testing. Treatment ranges from observation to surgery. Sudden severe pain with nausea may indicate torsion or rupture and requires urgent evaluation.','English'],
      ['Menopause symptoms','Internal Medicine','Meaning: declining ovarian hormone production may cause hot flashes, sleep disturbance, mood changes, vaginal dryness, and bone loss. Evaluation is usually clinical. Treatment may include lifestyle measures, nonhormonal medicines, local vaginal therapy, or individualized systemic hormone therapy after reviewing benefits and risks.','English'],
      ['Erectile dysfunction','Internal Medicine','Meaning: persistent difficulty achieving or maintaining an erection. Vascular disease, diabetes, medicines, low testosterone, neurologic disease, and psychological factors may contribute. Evaluation includes history, examination, cardiovascular risk, and selected labs. Treatment ranges from lifestyle and medication changes to tablets, devices, injections, or procedures.','English'],
      ['Enlarged prostate','Internal Medicine','Meaning: benign prostate growth can slow urine flow, cause urgency, or increase nighttime urination. Evaluation may include symptom score, examination, urinalysis, kidney function, and selected PSA testing. Treatment ranges from observation and medicines to office procedures or surgery. Inability to urinate is urgent.','English'],
      ['Prostatitis','Internal Medicine','Meaning: prostate inflammation may cause pelvic pain, painful urination, fever, or urinary symptoms. Bacterial infection causes some cases; chronic pelvic pain is often nonbacterial. Urine testing and examination guide care. Treatment may include antibiotics when bacterial, pain control, urinary medicines, and pelvic-floor therapy. Sepsis symptoms are urgent.','English'],
      ['Low testosterone','Internal Medicine','Meaning: testosterone is repeatedly low with compatible symptoms. Aging alone does not establish deficiency. Morning measurements, medication review, pituitary hormones, and cause-directed testing confirm it. Treatment addresses reversible causes and may include testosterone after discussing fertility, prostate, blood-count, cardiovascular, and monitoring considerations.','English'],
      ['Vitamin D deficiency','Internal Medicine','Meaning: vitamin D is below the level needed for bone and mineral health. Limited sun exposure, low intake, malabsorption, obesity, liver or kidney disease, and medicines contribute. A 25-hydroxyvitamin D test is used when indicated. Treatment uses individualized supplementation and correction of contributing conditions.','English'],
      ['Vitamin B12 deficiency','Blood','Meaning: inadequate B12 can cause anemia, numbness, balance trouble, or cognitive changes. Causes include autoimmune pernicious anemia, stomach or bowel disease, surgery, diet, and medicines. CBC, B12, methylmalonic acid, and cause-directed tests help diagnose it. Treatment uses oral or injected B12 depending on severity and absorption.','English'],
      ['Low sodium','Internal Medicine','Meaning: sodium concentration in the blood is low, usually from excess water relative to sodium. Medicines, vomiting, heart, liver, kidney, adrenal, and SIADH causes are considered. Serum and urine testing guide treatment. Correction must be controlled. Confusion, seizure, or severe symptoms requires emergency care.','English'],
      ['High potassium','Internal Medicine','Meaning: potassium is elevated and can affect heart rhythm. Kidney disease, medicines, tissue breakdown, acidosis, and sample error are common considerations. Repeat testing and ECG may be urgent. Treatment depends on level, ECG, and cause and may include heart protection, shifting potassium, removal, or dialysis.','English'],
      ['High calcium','Internal Medicine','Meaning: excess blood calcium may cause thirst, constipation, kidney stones, confusion, or rhythm problems. Hyperparathyroidism, cancer, supplements, medicines, and other disorders are causes. Repeat corrected or ionized calcium, PTH, kidney function, and cause-directed tests guide treatment. Severe symptomatic elevation is urgent.','English'],
      ['Childhood asthma','Internal Medicine','Childhood asthma explained through symptoms, triggers, inhaler use, monitoring, prevention, school planning, and warning signs that require urgent medical attention.','English'],
      ['High platelets','Blood','Meaning: thrombocytosis is an elevated platelet count. Infection, inflammation, iron deficiency, surgery, cancer, or a myeloproliferative neoplasm may cause it. CBC trend, smear, ferritin, inflammatory markers, and selected mutation testing guide evaluation. Treatment targets the cause and individualized clot or bleeding risk.','English'],
      ['Low white blood cells','Blood','Meaning: leukopenia is a reduced white-cell count, sometimes involving infection-fighting neutrophils. Viral illness, medicines, autoimmune disease, nutritional deficiency, and marrow disorders are causes. CBC differential, smear, trends, and targeted tests guide care. Fever with marked neutropenia requires emergency evaluation.','English'],
      ['High white blood cells','Blood','Meaning: leukocytosis is an elevated white-cell count. Infection, inflammation, stress, smoking, steroids, pregnancy, and marrow disease are possible causes. The differential, trend, symptoms, smear, and selected flow or molecular testing determine significance. Treatment is directed at the cause rather than the number alone.','English'],
      ['High hemoglobin','Blood','Meaning: erythrocytosis is excess red-cell concentration. Dehydration, smoking, low oxygen, testosterone, kidney signals, and polycythemia vera are causes. Repeat CBC, oxygen assessment, erythropoietin, JAK2 testing, and cause-directed studies guide diagnosis. Treatment addresses cause and clot risk; severe symptoms require prompt care.','English'],
      ['Elevated ferritin','Blood','Meaning: ferritin can rise from inflammation, liver disease, infection, metabolic disease, cancer, or iron overload. It does not equal iron overload by itself. Transferrin saturation, CBC, liver tests, inflammation markers, history, and selected genetic or imaging studies guide evaluation. Treatment targets the underlying cause.','English'],
      ['Elevated alkaline phosphatase','Internal Medicine','Meaning: alkaline phosphatase may come from liver, bile ducts, or bone. Obstruction, liver disease, healing fracture, bone turnover, pregnancy, and some cancers are causes. Other liver tests, GGT, calcium-related studies, and imaging help identify the source. Treatment depends on the diagnosis.','English'],
      ['Elevated bilirubin','Internal Medicine','Meaning: bilirubin rises from increased red-cell breakdown, impaired liver processing, liver injury, or bile obstruction. Direct and indirect fractions, CBC, hemolysis tests, liver enzymes, and ultrasound guide evaluation. Treatment targets the cause. Jaundice with fever, pain, confusion, or bleeding needs urgent care.','English'],
      ['Protein in urine','Internal Medicine','Meaning: albumin or other protein is leaking into urine. Diabetes, high blood pressure, infection, inflammation, and plasma-cell disorders are causes. Repeat urine albumin-to-creatinine or protein testing, urinalysis, kidney function, and selected blood studies guide care. Treatment controls the cause and protects kidney and cardiovascular health.','English'],
      ['Abnormal thyroid tests','Internal Medicine','Meaning: TSH and thyroid hormones do not follow the expected pattern. Thyroid disease, pregnancy, acute illness, supplements, medicines, and laboratory interference can contribute. Repeat testing and selected antibodies or imaging clarify the cause. Treatment depends on symptoms, persistence, pregnancy status, and the specific disorder.','English'],
      ['A1C test','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: hemoglobin A1C estimates average glucose over roughly two to three months. It helps diagnose and monitor diabetes but may be misleading with anemia, transfusion, kidney disease, pregnancy, or altered red-cell lifespan. Results are interpreted with glucose data. Treatment goals are individualized.','English'],
      ['CBC blood test','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: a complete blood count measures red cells, hemoglobin, white cells, and platelets. It helps evaluate anemia, infection, bleeding, inflammation, and marrow disease. Abnormal results are interpreted using the differential, indices, trends, symptoms, smear, and targeted follow-up rather than one isolated number.','English'],
      ['Comprehensive metabolic panel','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: this panel surveys glucose, electrolytes, kidney markers, proteins, and liver-related tests. It can reveal dehydration, organ dysfunction, or treatment effects but rarely makes a diagnosis alone. Results are interpreted with symptoms, medicines, trends, and targeted repeat or confirmatory testing.','English'],
      ['Iron studies','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: ferritin, serum iron, transferrin or TIBC, and saturation assess iron availability and storage. Low ferritin usually supports deficiency, while inflammation can raise ferritin. Results are interpreted with CBC, reticulocytes, symptoms, and the possible source of blood loss before choosing oral or intravenous replacement.','English'],
      ['PSA test','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: prostate-specific antigen is produced by prostate tissue. Cancer, benign enlargement, inflammation, recent procedures, and other factors can raise it. Age, trend, examination, risk, MRI, and biopsy considerations guide interpretation. PSA is not a diagnosis by itself, and screening decisions should be individualized.','English'],
      ['CA-125 test','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: CA-125 is a blood marker used mainly to follow selected ovarian cancers. Menstruation, endometriosis, fibroids, pregnancy, liver disease, and inflammation can raise it. It is not a stand-alone screening or diagnostic test for most people. Imaging, examination, pathology, and trends provide context.','English'],
      ['CEA test','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: carcinoembryonic antigen is used mainly to monitor selected cancers, especially colorectal cancer. Smoking, inflammation, liver disease, and other conditions can raise it. It does not diagnose cancer alone. Baseline context and trends during or after treatment are more informative than one isolated value.','English'],
      ['CT scan with contrast','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: CT uses X-rays and computer reconstruction; contrast can improve organ and vessel detail. Kidney function, allergy history, pregnancy, and the clinical question guide preparation. Benefits and radiation or contrast risks are individualized. Results must be interpreted with history, examination, and prior imaging.','English'],
      ['MRI scan','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: MRI uses magnetic fields and radiofrequency energy to create detailed images without ionizing radiation. Metal implants, claustrophobia, pregnancy, kidney function, and gadolinium use affect planning. It is especially useful for brain, spine, joints, pelvis, liver, and selected cancer questions.','English'],
      ['Ultrasound','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: ultrasound uses sound waves to examine organs, blood flow, pregnancy, and superficial structures without radiation. Preparation varies by body area. It is useful for many first-line evaluations but can be limited by depth, bowel gas, and operator factors. Further CT, MRI, or biopsy may still be needed.','English'],
      ['Colonoscopy','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: a flexible camera examines the colon and can remove polyps or obtain biopsies. It is used for screening and symptoms such as bleeding or iron deficiency. Bowel preparation quality affects accuracy. Sedation and procedural risks are reviewed beforehand. Pathology determines follow-up when tissue is removed.','English'],
      ['Upper endoscopy','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: a flexible camera examines the esophagus, stomach, and first small intestine. It evaluates swallowing trouble, bleeding, anemia, persistent pain, reflux alarms, or abnormal imaging. Biopsies can identify inflammation, infection, precancer, or cancer. Preparation, sedation, and risks are reviewed beforehand.','English'],
      ['Mammogram','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: low-dose breast X-ray can detect cancer before symptoms and evaluate some breast concerns. Screening timing depends on age and risk. Dense tissue may limit sensitivity and prompt additional imaging. An abnormal mammogram is not a cancer diagnosis; diagnostic imaging and sometimes biopsy establish the answer.','English'],
      ['Breast biopsy','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: tissue is sampled to determine whether a breast abnormality is benign, high risk, in situ cancer, or invasive cancer. Image-guided core biopsy is common. Pathology may test grade and estrogen, progesterone, and HER2 markers. These results guide surgery and systemic treatment decisions.','English'],
      ['Pap and HPV testing','Tests & Treatment','Meaning: cervical screening identifies high-risk HPV infection and abnormal cells before cancer develops. The test combination and interval depend on age, history, and immune status. Abnormal results may require repeat testing, colposcopy, or biopsy. HPV vaccination prevents many related cancers but does not replace screening.','English']
    ];
